Binary search finds a value in a sorted array by repeatedly halving the part that could still contain it. Each probe looks at the middle element and throws away the half that cannot hold the answer, so a million elements need about twenty probes.

## The invariant

Keep two indices, `lo` and `hi`, with one promise: *if the target is anywhere, it is in `a[lo..hi]`*. Probe `mid`. If `a[mid]` is too small the target can only be to its right, so `lo = mid + 1`; if it is too big, `hi = mid - 1`. When `lo` passes `hi` the range is empty and the target is absent.

:::viz{id=binary-search}

```python run
def binary_search(a, target):
    lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1
    probes = 0
    while lo <= hi:
        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2
        probes += 1
        if a[mid] == target:
            return mid, probes
        if a[mid] < target:
            lo = mid + 1
        else:
            hi = mid - 1
    return -1, probes

a = list(range(0, 2_000_000, 2))   # one million sorted even numbers
print(binary_search(a, 1_234_566))  # (index, probes)
print(binary_search(a, 7))          # odd, so absent
```

Each probe halves the range, so an array of n elements needs at most this many probes — 20 for a million, 30 for a billion:

$$
\lceil \log_2 (n + 1) \rceil
$$

:::callout{kind=complexity time="O(log n)" space="O(1)"}
Logarithmic time needs two things: random access (an array, not a linked list) and sorted data.
:::

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="Computing mid"}
`(lo + hi) / 2` can overflow a 32-bit integer in C++ or Java when both indices are large. `lo + (hi - lo) / 2` never does. Python integers cannot overflow, but the habit pays off in every other language.
:::

## The same search in C++

```cpp run title=search.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int search(const vector<int>& a, int target) {
    int lo = 0, hi = (int)a.size() - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (a[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> a = {3, 9, 14, 20, 27, 31, 38, 45, 52, 60, 71, 88};
    cout << search(a, 52) << " " << search(a, 50) << "\n";  // 8 -1
}
```

:::question{slug=binary-search}

Plain binary search answers "is it here?". The more useful form answers "where does it start?" — the first index whose value is at least `target`. That one question covers insert positions, counting occurrences, and searching over answers that are not stored in any array.

## A predicate that flips once

Picture a yes/no question about each index that reads *no, no, …, no, yes, …, yes* — it flips exactly once. For "the first index with `a[i] >= target`" the question is `a[i] >= target`. Binary search finds where it flips:

```python run
def lower_bound(a, target):
    lo, hi = 0, len(a)          # the answer is in [lo, hi]; len(a) means "none"
    while lo < hi:
        mid = (lo + hi) // 2
        if a[mid] >= target:
            hi = mid            # mid qualifies; the first one may be further left
        else:
            lo = mid + 1        # mid fails; the first one is to its right
    return lo

a = [1, 3, 3, 3, 8, 12]
print(lower_bound(a, 3))                      # 1: the first 3
print(lower_bound(a, 4))                      # 4: where 4 would be inserted
print(lower_bound(a, 20))                     # 6: past the end
print(lower_bound(a, 4) - lower_bound(a, 3))  # 3: how many 3s
```

The template cannot lose the answer: `hi` only ever moves onto an index that qualifies, `lo` only ever moves past one that does not, and the loop stops when they meet.

:::callout{kind=note title="Half-open ranges"}
Here `hi` starts at `len(a)`, one past the last index, and the loop runs while `lo < hi`. Mixing this with the `lo <= hi` / `hi = mid - 1` style from the previous lesson is how infinite loops happen — pick one template per function and stick to it.
:::

## A rotated array

A sorted array rotated at an unknown point, like `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]`, still has a predicate that flips once: "is `a[i]` at most the last element?" is false along the upper run and true from the minimum onward. So the minimum is simply the first index where it holds:

```javascript run
function findMin(a) {
  let lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (a[mid] <= a[a.length - 1]) hi = mid; // mid is in the lower run
    else lo = mid + 1;                        // mid is in the upper run
  }
  return a[lo];
}

console.log(findMin([4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2])); // 0
console.log(findMin([11, 13, 15, 17]));      // 11 (not rotated)
```

:::callout{kind=pitfall title="Read the result carefully"}
When the loop ends, `lo` is either an index that satisfies the predicate or `len(a)` when none does. Check for the second case before reading `a[lo]`.
:::

